class::Error
categories::Core
summary::superclass of all Errors

description::
Error and its subclasses separate different types of error is that can occur in the SuperCollider program into distinct classes, so that they can be reported differently to the user.

Anywhere that an error must be reported to the user and execution must stop, an error object must be created and thrown.
code::
(
var file, path = "betcha-this-file-doesnt-exist.txt";
if((file = File(path, "r")).isOpen) {
	"File is % bytes long.\n".postf(file.length);
	file.close;
} {
	Error("File % could not be opened.".format(path)).throw;
};
)
::

For backward compatibility, code::.die():: creates the error for you.

code::
"Keyboard not found. Press F1 to Resume.".die;
::

Throwing an error object gives the caller the opportunity to catch, and possibly recover from, the error. See the Exception help file for more information about this.

subsection::Error hierarchy

The following error classes exist in the main library.

list::
## link::Classes/Error::
## link::Classes/ErrorWrapper:: : wraps an existing error in another.
## link::Classes/DeprecatedError:: : this method is no longer supported.
## link::Classes/MethodError:: : generic error occurring within a method.
## link::Classes/DoesNotUnderstandError:: : the receiver does not understand the method name.
## link::Classes/BinaryOpFailureError:: : a binary operator cannot work with the operand classes.
## link::Classes/ImmutableError:: : attempted to modify an immutable object.
## link::Classes/MustBeBooleanError:: : a test (in if or while) returned a non-Boolean value.
## link::Classes/NotYetImplementedError:: : the method name exists, but isn't implemented yet.
## link::Classes/OutOfContextReturnError:: : a method return by ^ took place outside of a method.
## link::Classes/PrimitiveFailedError:: : an error occurred inside a primitive.
## link::Classes/ShouldNotImplementError:: : you called a method on a class that has no business implementing it.
## link::Classes/SubclassResponsibilityError:: : you called a method on an abstract class.
::

The exact inheritance tree looks like this:
classtree::Error


subsection::Changing how a custom error is displayed

The only reason you should ever need to write an new type of error is if you intend to catch it and do some custom recovery logic with it.
Otherwise, you just should use link::Classes/Error::

There are two places you can change how an error is displayed, when it is constructed, and through overloading two specific methods.

If you only need to change the constructor arguments, consider using link::Classes/Error:: directly.

The constructor of code::Error:: takes 4 arguments. 

First, a string saying what went wrong, this should describe the type of error.

Second, code::callFrameAnnotations::, this is an array of code::nil:: or strings. Working up the call stack, the text is shown text to the source code.
This is best shown by example.

code::
MyError : Error {
	*new { |what| 
		^super.new(what, [
			"Here is where we called throw on the error.",
			"This is the call above that, in this case, it is where we invoked `g`!",
			"And finally, this is where we invoked the function `f`.",
		]);
	}
}

g = { var foo = 10; MyError("what??").throw };
f = { var bar = \meow; g.(); }; 
f.()
::

This produced an error report like this... remember, in the post window, this is read bottom to top!

code::
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ERROR: what??

3   : Anonymous Function
          file://interpreted_text:3:5
             1 │ g = { var foo = 10; MyError("what??").throw };
             2 │ f = { var bar = \meow; g.(); };
             3 │ f.()
               ┆ ^^^^ And finally, this is where we invoked the function `f`.
2   : Function Name: 'f'
       vars: bar='meow'
          file://interpreted_text:2:28
             1 │ g = { var foo = 10; MyError("what??").throw };
             2 │ f = { var bar = \meow; g.(); };
               ┆                        ^^^^ This is the call above that, in this case, it is where we invoked `g`!
             3 │ f.()
1   : Function Name: 'g'
       vars: foo=10
          file://interpreted_text:1:44
             1 │ g = { var foo = 10; MyError("what??").throw };
               ┆                     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ Here is where we called throw on the error.
             2 │ f = { var bar = \meow; g.(); };
             3 │ f.()


──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::

Third, code::methodBeforeBacktraceStart:: this is a function that returns true when encountering a method in the call trace which we would like to start printing after.
The simplest example is skipping everything before the throw method of the exception (the default), but it the case of link::Classes/DoesNotUnderstandError:: it is also used to skip code::Object:doesNotUnderstand:: too.

Fourth, code::MethodBacktraceEnd:: this is a function that return true when encountering the method one past the final backtrace we would like to print.
The default here will stop printing when we get to a code::try::, code::protect::, or go up into the interpreter methods.


When it comes to overriding methods, the two (and only two) that are allowed are code::reportStage1:: and code::reportStage2::. 
These are responsible for printing the backtrace and adding any additional information.
If you only want to add additionally information at the end of the report, you can just overload code::reportStage2::.
